{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '35,145p'; jq 'keys' paper_data.json; jq '{summary, dangling: [.references[]? | select(.status==\\\"dangling\\\")]}' scientific-evidence/verification-report.json; jq 'del(.claims)' claims_grounding.json; jq 'keys' validation_report.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '35,145p'; jq 'keys' paper_data.json; jq '{summary, dangling: [.references[]? | select(.status==\\\"dangling\\\")]}' scientific-evidence/verification-report.json; jq 'del(.claims)' claims_grounding.json; jq 'keys' validation_report.json\"","aggregated_output":"    35\t\\section{Introduction}\n    36\tRandomized experiments on connected populations have an estimand problem as well as an estimation problem. An individual assigned to control can still be affected by treated neighbors. Consequently, a contrast between the observed treatment arms need not estimate what a platform would obtain by treating everyone rather than no one. Potential-outcome formulations make the intervention explicit \\citep{Neyman1990,Rubin1974}; under interference, the outcome must depend on a vector of assignments rather than only the individual's assignment \\citep{Hudgens2008,Manski2013}. Network experimentation at scale makes this distinction practically relevant \\citep{Karrer2021}.\n    37\t\n    38\tA natural response is to regress outcomes on treatment, covariates, and summaries of neighbors' assignments, and evaluate the fitted function at the two global interventions \\citep{Chin2019}. This approach separates observed experimental data from the intervention of interest, but introduces a delicate extrapolation problem. A model can predict outcomes accurately near the observed treatment saturation while predicting the difference between saturation extremes poorly. Conversely, restricting a regression to a correctly specified low-dimensional exposure model can be more effective than adding flexible terms and regularization. Model complexity is not automatically useful for the target contrast.\n    39\t\n    40\tWe examine whether tuning regularization toward the endpoint contrast is sufficient to make a richer one-hop regression competitive. The estimator uses a quadratic exposure reference and a finite ridge grid. Its risk criterion is motivated by a fixed-design linear-model calculation, not by a general identification result. The comparison is deliberately anchored to meaningful simple alternatives: difference in means, covariate-adjusted own-treatment regression, and linear one-hop ordinary least squares (OLS). The scientific question is how their errors change across network structures, interference mechanisms, and assignment designs, rather than whether a single aggregate evaluation measure is high.\n    41\t\n    42\tThe evidence yields a negative answer to general superiority. Across the fixed benchmark, endpoint tuning substantially reduces aggregate error relative to assignment-only analysis but loses to linear one-hop OLS. The latter comparison is essential: a gain over a baseline that omits spillovers is not evidence that a more elaborate exposure regression is preferable. Threshold and two-hop conditions also show that small residuals on observed assignments do not certify accurate global intervention prediction.\n    43\t\n    44\tOur contributions are a concrete endpoint-functional tuning construction, a matched comparison across the complete recorded condition matrix, and an analysis of its observed limitations. We report Monte Carlo uncertainty at the seed-block level and distinguish it from causal confidence-interval coverage. Because feature expansion and tuning are not separately ablated, the results compare complete estimators; they do not attribute error differences to the tuning objective alone. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes the distinction that organizes the study.\n    45\t\n    46\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    47\t\\centering\n    48\t\\includegraphics[width=.98\\textwidth]{figures/endpoint_overview.png}\n    49\t\\caption{The target is an intervention contrast, not observed prediction error. A graph, covariates, observed assignment, and observed outcomes determine the fitted regression. All-treated and all-control assignments are separate prediction inputs; their outcomes are withheld from fitting. Algebraic estimability of the fitted contrast does not establish exposure support or robustness to misspecification.}\n    50\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    51\t\\end{figure}\n    52\t\n    53\t\\section{Related Work}\n    54\t\\paragraph{Interference and experimental design.}\n    55\t\\citet{Aronow2017} distinguish the assignment design, exposure mapping, and causal estimand in a randomization-based framework. Graph cluster randomization changes exposure probabilities and can improve design-based estimation under suitable exposure assumptions \\citep{Ugander2013}. Design and analysis can both reduce interference bias without eliminating the dependence on modeling assumptions \\citep{Eckles2017}. Staggered rollout provides another route under low-order interaction assumptions without requiring the graph \\citep{Cortez2022}. These are distinct designs and inferential targets; our static assignment comparisons do not evaluate their complete estimators or variance guarantees.\n    56\t\n    57\t\\paragraph{Regression adjustment.}\n    58\tGlobal intervention prediction from regression is established by \\citet{Chin2019}. Graph- and treatment-based covariate generation and selection, together with block-bootstrap inference, are studied by \\citet{Han2023}. We do not introduce regression adjustment for interference or compare against that full covariate-selection procedure. Instead, we evaluate a small, explicitly specified one-hop reference. The broader regression-adjustment literature cautions against treating model fitting as automatically beneficial \\citep{Freedman2008}; favorable asymptotic results require attention to interactions and variance estimation \\citep{Lin2013}. Those results under individualistic response cannot be transferred directly to the present endpoint-extrapolation setting.\n    59\t\n    60\t\\paragraph{Regularization and assessment.}\n    61\tRidge regression addresses unstable estimation with correlated predictors \\citep{Hoerl1970}. Generalized cross-validation targets prediction through a rotation-invariant criterion \\citep{Golub1979}. Unbiased risk estimation motivates target-specific assessment \\citep{Stein1981}, but apparent error can be optimistic when the same data are used for fitting and evaluation \\citep{Efron1986}. We distinguish a fixed-penalty identity from the selected minimum. Estimable linear functions are a linear-algebraic notion \\citep{Searle1965}; neither estimability nor heteroskedasticity-consistent variance estimation \\citep{White1980} alone addresses a missing spillover mechanism. Our bootstrap summarizes repeated experimental realizations \\citep{Efron1979}, not unmeasured inferential coverage. Finally, the graph families represent block structure \\citep{Choi2012}, preferential attachment \\citep{Barabasi1999}, small-world structure \\citep{Watts1998}, and a public social-network topology \\citep{Traud2012,Rossi2015}; they are not a random sample of online platforms.\n    62\t\n    63\t\\section{Method}\n    64\t\\subsection{Problem Setup and Design Rationale}\n    65\tFor an undirected graph with adjacency matrix $A$, let $d_i=\\sum_j A_{ij}$ and define the one-hop exposure and length-two exposure by\n    66\t\\begin{equation}\n    67\te_i(z)=\\frac{\\sum_j A_{ij}z_j}{\\max(d_i,1)},\\qquad\n    68\th_i(z)=\\frac{\\sum_j A_{ij}e_j(z)}{\\max(d_i,1)}.\n    69\t\\label{eq:exposure}\n    70\t\\end{equation}\n    71\tThe second expression averages neighbors' exposures and includes backtracking walks; it is not the fraction of distinct distance-two neighbors treated. Isolates retain exposure zero. Given fixed covariates and a fixed noise realization, the finite-population target is\n    72\t\\begin{equation}\n    73\t\\gate=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_i\\{Y_i(\\mathbf{1})-Y_i(\\mathbf{0})\\},\\qquad\n    74\t\\widehat\\gate=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_i\\{\\widehat f_i(\\mathbf{1})-\\widehat f_i(\\mathbf{0})\\}.\n    75\t\\label{eq:target}\n    76\t\\end{equation}\n    77\tOnly the graph, covariates $x_i$, realized treatment vector $z$, and $Y_i(z)$ are available during fitting. Endpoint outcomes and the target effect are not training feedback. We study point-estimation error, not recovery of every potential outcome.\n    78\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L676-L694]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L180-L213]]\n    79\t\n    80\tThe design rationale is that regularization should account for the particular linear functional in Equation~\\ref{eq:target}, rather than only average prediction near observed exposures. Our working hypothesis is conditional: an adequate and estimable reference can support endpoint-oriented bias--variance tuning. It does not imply that a richer reference improves on correctly specified one-hop OLS, or that one-hop features recover omitted multi-hop effects.\n    81\t\n    82\t\\subsection{Endpoint-Tuned Exposure Regression}\n    83\tThe inspected estimator source associated with the frozen evaluation constructs the reference\n    84\t\\begin{equation}\n    85\t\\phi_i(z)=\\bigl[1,\\ x_i^\\top,\\ z_i,\\ e_i(z),\\ z_i e_i(z),\\ c_i(z),\\ z_i c_i(z)\\bigr],\n    86\t\\qquad c_i(z)=e_i(z)\\{1-e_i(z)\\}.\n    87\t\\label{eq:features}\n    88\t\\end{equation}\n    89\tThe curvature terms vanish at exposure extremes but affect the observed fit. All non-intercept columns are centered and scaled using observed-assignment means and population standard deviations. Zero scales are replaced by one. Endpoint rows use this same saved transformation; the intercept is unpenalized. The complete source is provided as \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator.py}, functions \\texttt{\\_reference\\_features} and \\texttt{\\_fit\\_endpoint\\_sure}.\n    90\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L83-L160]]\n    91\t\n    92\tLet $W$ be the standardized non-intercept design, $y_c=y-\\bar y\\mathbf{1}$, and $q$ the difference between the mean transformed endpoint rows. For retained singular values, write $W=USV^\\top$ and $v=V^\\top q$. The reference contrast and each ridge contrast have weights\n    93\t\\begin{equation}\n    94\ta_0=US^{-1}v,\\qquad a_\\lambda=US(S^2+\\lambda I)^{-1}v,\\qquad\n    95\t\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda=a_\\lambda^\\top y_c.\n    96\t\\label{eq:weights}\n    97\t\\end{equation}\n    98\tHere $V$ contains retained right singular vectors, so $q$ must lie in their span. The residual variance estimate is $\\widehat\\sigma^2=\\|(I-UU^\\top)y_c\\|^2/(n-r-1)$, where $r$ is retained rank. The criterion is\n    99\t\\begin{equation}\n   100\t\\widehat R(\\lambda)=\n   101\t(\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda-\\widehat\\gate_0)^2\n   102\t+2\\widehat\\sigma^2 a_\\lambda^\\top a_0-\\widehat\\sigma^2\\|a_0\\|^2,\n   103\t\\quad\n   104\t\\lambda\\in n\\{0,10^{-6},10^{-4},10^{-2},1\\}.\n   105\t\\label{eq:risk}\n   106\t\\end{equation}\n   107\tThe smallest criterion value is selected, with ties resolved by grid order. This is a finite observed-data rule, not access to the true endpoint error.\n   108\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L114-L160]]\n   109\t\n   110\tUnder a correct fixed linear reference with independent homoskedastic Gaussian errors, $a_0^\\top E[y_c]=\\gate$ and $E[\\widehat\\sigma^2]=\\sigma^2$. For a \\emph{fixed} penalty, the expectation of the squared difference in Equation~\\ref{eq:risk} is the squared bias of $\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda$ plus $\\sigma^2\\|a_\\lambda-a_0\\|^2$. The remaining terms convert this variance contribution to $\\sigma^2\\|a_\\lambda\\|^2$. This calculation motivates the criterion; it is not a distribution-free theorem about network effects. Choosing the smallest estimated risk does not make that minimum an unbiased estimate of the selected estimator's risk. Misspecification also invalidates the premise that the reference contrast is unbiased.\n   111\t\n   112\tThe implementation checks finite inputs and SVD output, target row-space membership, and positive residual degrees of freedom. An unusable reference falls back to a fixed-ridge one-hop hinge regression; exactly constant outcomes bypass fitting and yield zero contrast. These safeguards prevent some algebraic or behavioral failures. They do not establish endpoint support, identify arbitrary interference, or supply a causal confidence interval. Exact numerical tolerances and the fallback basis appear in Supplementary Information.\n   113\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L69-L81]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L107-L145]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L199-L213]]\n   114\t\n   115\t\\subsection{Compared Approaches}\n   116\tDifference in means estimates the observed treated-minus-control mean outcome. Own-treatment OLS fits $[1,x_i^\\top,z_i]$ and uses the treatment coefficient. One-hop OLS fits $[1,x_i^\\top,z_i,e_i(z)]$ and predicts the global contrast. The latter equals the treatment coefficient plus the exposure coefficient multiplied by the fraction of nonisolated nodes. These baseline estimates are recorded on the same scientific cases as the endpoint-tuned estimator, so comparisons are paired rather than comparisons of separate simulation suites.\n   117\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L371-L392]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   118\t\n   119\tNo component ablation separates the quadratic reference from endpoint-risk tuning. In particular, the evidence contains no completed same-reference predictive-tuning comparison. We therefore evaluate complete configurations, not an isolated causal effect of changing the tuning objective. Constant prediction and copying observed outcomes are behavioral controls, not substantive alternatives for estimating the global effect.\n   120\t\n   121\t\\subsection{Experimental Protocol}\n   122\tThe fixed evaluation crosses four graph families and six conditions with 24 seed blocks, yielding 576 scientific records. Synthetic graphs have 4,096 nodes. The stochastic block model (SBM) has four blocks with within/between connection probabilities 0.04/0.002; Barab\\'asi--Albert (BA) uses five attachment edges; Watts--Strogatz (WS) uses neighborhood size 10 and rewiring probability 0.15. Stanford3 is a fixed, undirected public graph with 11,586 nodes and 568,309 edges. Synthetic topologies vary across seeds; Stanford3 repeats assignments and synthetic outcomes on one topology. Covariates have one column. The recorded generator uses unit-standard-deviation Gaussian noise, held fixed across counterfactual assignments within a realization.\n   123\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/generator]] @evidence[[source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/records]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json]] @evidence[[reproducibility/resources/final/benchmark.npz]]\n   124\t\n   125\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   126\t\\centering\n   127\t\\caption{Outcome and assignment conditions. All outcomes are $Y_i(z)=0.5x_i+z_i+s_i(z)+\\epsilon_i$. Individual treatment is independent Bernoulli with probability $1/2$. The cluster condition changes assignment while retaining the strong-linear outcome mechanism. The recorded global effect is identical across the four families for each condition.}\n   128\t\\label{tab:protocol}\n   129\t\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n   130\t\\toprule\n   131\tCondition & Spillover $s_i(z)$ & Assignment & $\\gate$\\\\\n   132\t\\midrule\n   133\tNo spill & $0$ & Individual & 1\\\\\n   134\tWeak linear & $0.25e_i$ & Individual & 1.25\\\\\n   135\tStrong linear & $2e_i$ & Individual & 3\\\\\n   136\tThreshold & $2\\mathbf{1}\\{e_i\\geq0.75\\}+z_i e_i$ & Individual & 4\\\\\n   137\tTwo-hop & $2h_i$ & Individual & 3\\\\\n   138\tCluster & $2e_i$ & Cluster & 3\\\\\n   139\t\\bottomrule\n   140\t\\end{tabular}\n   141\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L686-L693]] @evidence[[source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/mechanisms]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   142\t\\end{table}\n   143\t\n   144\tTable~\\ref{tab:protocol} specifies the outcome mechanisms. The cluster design forms 32 deterministic graph-BFS clusters with independent cluster treatment probability $1/2$; it is not optimized for this estimator. The smallest unassigned node starts each traversal; neighbors are sorted, each cluster receives at most $\\lceil n/32\\rceil$ nodes, and disconnected frontiers restart at the smallest unassigned node. Assignment designs share topology, covariates, and noise within a seed/family, but this is not a controlled intervention on exposure support alone.\n   145\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/mechanisms]]\n[\n  \"ablation_studies\",\n  \"all_programs\",\n  \"best_any\",\n  \"best_correct\",\n  \"evidence_sources\",\n  \"final_evaluation\",\n  \"generated_at\",\n  \"grounding_sources\",\n  \"idea_titles\",\n  \"improvement_trace\",\n  \"paper_profile\",\n  \"presentation_contract\",\n  \"results_dir\",\n  \"top_programs\",\n  \"totals\"\n]\n{\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"anchored_references\": 79,\n    \"dangling_references\": 1,\n    \"manifests_checked\": 22,\n    \"resolved_references\": 113,\n    \"total_references\": 193\n  },\n  \"dangling\": []\n}\n{}\n[\n  \"bibtex_key_count\",\n  \"citation_count\",\n  \"claim_count\",\n  \"error\",\n  \"evidence_validation\",\n  \"feedback\",\n  \"figure_validation\",\n  \"latex_logs\",\n  \"missing_sections\",\n  \"ok\",\n  \"page_validation\",\n  \"paper_dir\",\n  \"paper_profile\",\n  \"pdf_path\",\n  \"reference_validation\",\n  \"standalone_language_validation\",\n  \"supplementary_information_validation\",\n  \"venue_validation\",\n  \"watermark_validation\"\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '53,114p' paper.tex; jq '{final_evaluation: (.final_evaluation|del(.evaluation_evidence)), final_qualification: (.grounding_sources|with_entries(select(.key|startswith(\\\"final.qualification\\\")))), totals, downstream_study}' paper_data.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '53,114p' paper.tex; jq '{final_evaluation: (.final_evaluation|del(.evaluation_evidence)), final_qualification: (.grounding_sources|with_entries(select(.key|startswith(\\\"final.qualification\\\")))), totals, downstream_study}' paper_data.json\"","aggregated_output":"\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Interference and experimental design.}\n\\citet{Aronow2017} distinguish the assignment design, exposure mapping, and causal estimand in a randomization-based framework. Graph cluster randomization changes exposure probabilities and can improve design-based estimation under suitable exposure assumptions \\citep{Ugander2013}. Design and analysis can both reduce interference bias without eliminating the dependence on modeling assumptions \\citep{Eckles2017}. Staggered rollout provides another route under low-order interaction assumptions without requiring the graph \\citep{Cortez2022}. These are distinct designs and inferential targets; our static assignment comparisons do not evaluate their complete estimators or variance guarantees.\n\n\\paragraph{Regression adjustment.}\nGlobal intervention prediction from regression is established by \\citet{Chin2019}. Graph- and treatment-based covariate generation and selection, together with block-bootstrap inference, are studied by \\citet{Han2023}. We do not introduce regression adjustment for interference or compare against that full covariate-selection procedure. Instead, we evaluate a small, explicitly specified one-hop reference. The broader regression-adjustment literature cautions against treating model fitting as automatically beneficial \\citep{Freedman2008}; favorable asymptotic results require attention to interactions and variance estimation \\citep{Lin2013}. Those results under individualistic response cannot be transferred directly to the present endpoint-extrapolation setting.\n\n\\paragraph{Regularization and assessment.}\nRidge regression addresses unstable estimation with correlated predictors \\citep{Hoerl1970}. Generalized cross-validation targets prediction through a rotation-invariant criterion \\citep{Golub1979}. Unbiased risk estimation motivates target-specific assessment \\citep{Stein1981}, but apparent error can be optimistic when the same data are used for fitting and evaluation \\citep{Efron1986}. We distinguish a fixed-penalty identity from the selected minimum. Estimable linear functions are a linear-algebraic notion \\citep{Searle1965}; neither estimability nor heteroskedasticity-consistent variance estimation \\citep{White1980} alone addresses a missing spillover mechanism. Our bootstrap summarizes repeated experimental realizations \\citep{Efron1979}, not unmeasured inferential coverage. Finally, the graph families represent block structure \\citep{Choi2012}, preferential attachment \\citep{Barabasi1999}, small-world structure \\citep{Watts1998}, and a public social-network topology \\citep{Traud2012,Rossi2015}; they are not a random sample of online platforms.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem Setup and Design Rationale}\nFor an undirected graph with adjacency matrix $A$, let $d_i=\\sum_j A_{ij}$ and define the one-hop exposure and length-two exposure by\n\\begin{equation}\ne_i(z)=\\frac{\\sum_j A_{ij}z_j}{\\max(d_i,1)},\\qquad\nh_i(z)=\\frac{\\sum_j A_{ij}e_j(z)}{\\max(d_i,1)}.\n\\label{eq:exposure}\n\\end{equation}\nThe second expression averages neighbors' exposures and includes backtracking walks; it is not the fraction of distinct distance-two neighbors treated. Isolates retain exposure zero. Given fixed covariates and a fixed noise realization, the finite-population target is\n\\begin{equation}\n\\gate=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_i\\{Y_i(\\mathbf{1})-Y_i(\\mathbf{0})\\},\\qquad\n\\widehat\\gate=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_i\\{\\widehat f_i(\\mathbf{1})-\\widehat f_i(\\mathbf{0})\\}.\n\\label{eq:target}\n\\end{equation}\nOnly the graph, covariates $x_i$, realized treatment vector $z$, and $Y_i(z)$ are available during fitting. Endpoint outcomes and the target effect are not training feedback. We study point-estimation error, not recovery of every potential outcome.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L676-L694]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L180-L213]]\n\nThe design rationale is that regularization should account for the particular linear functional in Equation~\\ref{eq:target}, rather than only average prediction near observed exposures. Our working hypothesis is conditional: an adequate and estimable reference can support endpoint-oriented bias--variance tuning. It does not imply that a richer reference improves on correctly specified one-hop OLS, or that one-hop features recover omitted multi-hop effects.\n\n\\subsection{Endpoint-Tuned Exposure Regression}\nThe inspected estimator source associated with the frozen evaluation constructs the reference\n\\begin{equation}\n\\phi_i(z)=\\bigl[1,\\ x_i^\\top,\\ z_i,\\ e_i(z),\\ z_i e_i(z),\\ c_i(z),\\ z_i c_i(z)\\bigr],\n\\qquad c_i(z)=e_i(z)\\{1-e_i(z)\\}.\n\\label{eq:features}\n\\end{equation}\nThe curvature terms vanish at exposure extremes but affect the observed fit. All non-intercept columns are centered and scaled using observed-assignment means and population standard deviations. Zero scales are replaced by one. Endpoint rows use this same saved transformation; the intercept is unpenalized. The complete source is provided as \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator.py}, functions \\texttt{\\_reference\\_features} and \\texttt{\\_fit\\_endpoint\\_sure}.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L83-L160]]\n\nLet $W$ be the standardized non-intercept design, $y_c=y-\\bar y\\mathbf{1}$, and $q$ the difference between the mean transformed endpoint rows. For retained singular values, write $W=USV^\\top$ and $v=V^\\top q$. The reference contrast and each ridge contrast have weights\n\\begin{equation}\na_0=US^{-1}v,\\qquad a_\\lambda=US(S^2+\\lambda I)^{-1}v,\\qquad\n\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda=a_\\lambda^\\top y_c.\n\\label{eq:weights}\n\\end{equation}\nHere $V$ contains retained right singular vectors, so $q$ must lie in their span. The residual variance estimate is $\\widehat\\sigma^2=\\|(I-UU^\\top)y_c\\|^2/(n-r-1)$, where $r$ is retained rank. The criterion is\n\\begin{equation}\n\\widehat R(\\lambda)=\n(\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda-\\widehat\\gate_0)^2\n+2\\widehat\\sigma^2 a_\\lambda^\\top a_0-\\widehat\\sigma^2\\|a_0\\|^2,\n\\quad\n\\lambda\\in n\\{0,10^{-6},10^{-4},10^{-2},1\\}.\n\\label{eq:risk}\n\\end{equation}\nThe smallest criterion value is selected, with ties resolved by grid order. This is a finite observed-data rule, not access to the true endpoint error.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L114-L160]]\n\nUnder a correct fixed linear reference with independent homoskedastic Gaussian errors, $a_0^\\top E[y_c]=\\gate$ and $E[\\widehat\\sigma^2]=\\sigma^2$. For a \\emph{fixed} penalty, the expectation of the squared difference in Equation~\\ref{eq:risk} is the squared bias of $\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda$ plus $\\sigma^2\\|a_\\lambda-a_0\\|^2$. The remaining terms convert this variance contribution to $\\sigma^2\\|a_\\lambda\\|^2$. This calculation motivates the criterion; it is not a distribution-free theorem about network effects. Choosing the smallest estimated risk does not make that minimum an unbiased estimate of the selected estimator's risk. Misspecification also invalidates the premise that the reference contrast is unbiased.\n\nThe implementation checks finite inputs and SVD output, target row-space membership, and positive residual degrees of freedom. An unusable reference falls back to a fixed-ridge one-hop hinge regression; exactly constant outcomes bypass fitting and yield zero contrast. These safeguards prevent some algebraic or behavioral failures. They do not establish endpoint support, identify arbitrary interference, or supply a causal confidence interval. Exact numerical tolerances and the fallback basis appear in Supplementary Information.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L69-L81]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L107-L145]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L199-L213]]\n\n{\n  \"final_evaluation\": {\n    \"correct\": true,\n    \"evaluator_sha256\": \"sha256:273cf370e3c2aaeb50a4659fbb1c8cff99d1d042bc6afa02f90594cef531e5e3\",\n    \"manifest_path\": \"work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\",\n    \"metrics\": {\n      \"combined_score\": 0.9931177679506574,\n      \"control_success_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"failed_episode_fraction\": 0.0,\n      \"mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias\": 0.4597691498768297,\n      \"mean_capped_normalized_rmse\": 0.6140475146408374\n    },\n    \"outcome\": \"qualified\",\n    \"program_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:37121e22c23552d3c9168e22661f12879935b238b6838ce94dc038a7eaa15e72\",\n    \"results_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3c3bcb43740a1d90e9761ad07a965974af2ceb71ec65e586784027ba8e9f049a\",\n    \"status\": \"completed\",\n    \"task_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:2697fa6dc4a81915db3cf9aaa0a6298cb5df2b6a99acb60e47c825617266184f\"\n  },\n  \"final_qualification\": {\n    \"final.qualification.correct\": {\n      \"evaluation_scope\": \"sealed_final\",\n      \"id\": \"final.qualification.correct\",\n      \"manifest_path\": \"work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\",\n      \"program_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:37121e22c23552d3c9168e22661f12879935b238b6838ce94dc038a7eaa15e72\",\n      \"results_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3c3bcb43740a1d90e9761ad07a965974af2ceb71ec65e586784027ba8e9f049a\",\n      \"type\": \"final_qualification\",\n      \"value\": true\n    },\n    \"final.qualification.outcome\": {\n      \"evaluation_scope\": \"sealed_final\",\n      \"id\": \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n      \"manifest_path\": \"work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\",\n      \"program_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:37121e22c23552d3c9168e22661f12879935b238b6838ce94dc038a7eaa15e72\",\n      \"results_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3c3bcb43740a1d90e9761ad07a965974af2ceb71ec65e586784027ba8e9f049a\",\n      \"type\": \"final_qualification\",\n      \"value\": \"qualified\"\n    },\n    \"final.qualification.status\": {\n      \"evaluation_scope\": \"sealed_final\",\n      \"id\": \"final.qualification.status\",\n      \"manifest_path\": \"work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\",\n      \"program_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:37121e22c23552d3c9168e22661f12879935b238b6838ce94dc038a7eaa15e72\",\n      \"results_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3c3bcb43740a1d90e9761ad07a965974af2ceb71ec65e586784027ba8e9f049a\",\n      \"type\": \"final_qualification\",\n      \"value\": \"completed\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"totals\": {\n    \"attempts\": 3,\n    \"correct_programs\": 2,\n    \"evaluated_programs\": 2,\n    \"ideas\": 3\n  },\n  \"downstream_study\": null\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
