{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '80,114p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '147,201p'\"","aggregated_output":"    80\tThe design rationale is that regularization should account for the particular linear functional in Equation~\\ref{eq:target}, rather than only average prediction near observed exposures. Our working hypothesis is conditional: an adequate and estimable reference can support endpoint-oriented bias--variance tuning. It does not imply that a richer reference improves on correctly specified one-hop OLS, or that one-hop features recover omitted multi-hop effects.\n    81\t\n    82\t\\subsection{Endpoint-Tuned Exposure Regression}\n    83\tThe inspected estimator source associated with the frozen evaluation constructs the reference\n    84\t\\begin{equation}\n    85\t\\phi_i(z)=\\bigl[1,\\ x_i^\\top,\\ z_i,\\ e_i(z),\\ z_i e_i(z),\\ c_i(z),\\ z_i c_i(z)\\bigr],\n    86\t\\qquad c_i(z)=e_i(z)\\{1-e_i(z)\\}.\n    87\t\\label{eq:features}\n    88\t\\end{equation}\n    89\tThe curvature terms vanish at exposure extremes but affect the observed fit. All non-intercept columns are centered and scaled using observed-assignment means and population standard deviations. Zero scales are replaced by one. Endpoint rows use this same saved transformation; the intercept is unpenalized. The complete source is provided as \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator.py}, functions \\texttt{\\_reference\\_features} and \\texttt{\\_fit\\_endpoint\\_sure}.\n    90\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L83-L160]]\n    91\t\n    92\tLet $W$ be the standardized non-intercept design, $y_c=y-\\bar y\\mathbf{1}$, and $q$ the difference between the mean transformed endpoint rows. For retained singular values, write $W=USV^\\top$ and $v=V^\\top q$. The reference contrast and each ridge contrast have weights\n    93\t\\begin{equation}\n    94\ta_0=US^{-1}v,\\qquad a_\\lambda=US(S^2+\\lambda I)^{-1}v,\\qquad\n    95\t\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda=a_\\lambda^\\top y_c.\n    96\t\\label{eq:weights}\n    97\t\\end{equation}\n    98\tHere $V$ contains retained right singular vectors, so $q$ must lie in their span. The residual variance estimate is $\\widehat\\sigma^2=\\|(I-UU^\\top)y_c\\|^2/(n-r-1)$, where $r$ is retained rank. The criterion is\n    99\t\\begin{equation}\n   100\t\\widehat R(\\lambda)=\n   101\t(\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda-\\widehat\\gate_0)^2\n   102\t+2\\widehat\\sigma^2 a_\\lambda^\\top a_0-\\widehat\\sigma^2\\|a_0\\|^2,\n   103\t\\quad\n   104\t\\lambda\\in n\\{0,10^{-6},10^{-4},10^{-2},1\\}.\n   105\t\\label{eq:risk}\n   106\t\\end{equation}\n   107\tThe smallest criterion value is selected, with ties resolved by grid order. This is a finite observed-data rule, not access to the true endpoint error.\n   108\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L114-L160]]\n   109\t\n   110\tUnder a correct fixed linear reference with independent homoskedastic Gaussian errors, $a_0^\\top E[y_c]=\\gate$ and $E[\\widehat\\sigma^2]=\\sigma^2$. For a \\emph{fixed} penalty, the expectation of the squared difference in Equation~\\ref{eq:risk} is the squared bias of $\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda$ plus $\\sigma^2\\|a_\\lambda-a_0\\|^2$. The remaining terms convert this variance contribution to $\\sigma^2\\|a_\\lambda\\|^2$. This calculation motivates the criterion; it is not a distribution-free theorem about network effects. Choosing the smallest estimated risk does not make that minimum an unbiased estimate of the selected estimator's risk. Misspecification also invalidates the premise that the reference contrast is unbiased.\n   111\t\n   112\tThe implementation checks finite inputs and SVD output, target row-space membership, and positive residual degrees of freedom. An unusable reference falls back to a fixed-ridge one-hop hinge regression; exactly constant outcomes bypass fitting and yield zero contrast. These safeguards prevent some algebraic or behavioral failures. They do not establish endpoint support, identify arbitrary interference, or supply a causal confidence interval. Exact numerical tolerances and the fallback basis appear in Supplementary Information.\n   113\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L69-L81]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L107-L145]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L199-L213]]\n   114\t\n   147\tWe compute signed error $u_{sfkm}=\\widehat\\gate_{sfkm}-\\gate_{sfk}$ for seed $s$, family $f$, condition $k$, and method $m$. Cell RMSE is $(S^{-1}\\sum_s u_{sfkm}^2)^{1/2}$; cell absolute bias is $|S^{-1}\\sum_s u_{sfkm}|$. Aggregate values average these cell summaries equally across families and conditions. This gives each condition equal scientific weight rather than weighting by node count or treating all squared errors as one pooled RMSE. Both bias and RMSE matter: a small bias may mask large dispersion, whereas systematic endpoint misspecification can dominate both.\n   148\t\n   149\tThe evaluator caps signed errors at magnitude 10 in its normalized summaries; noise scale is one. No scientific estimate was clipped, and all scientific cases completed, so the reported raw endpoint-tuned summaries equal the retained capped summaries. Missing or failed observations would not be treated as measured losses in a separate estimator comparison. No new scientific simulations were executed for this manuscript.\n   150\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/aggregation]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n   151\t\n   152\tUncertainty uses 10,000 percentile bootstrap resamples of the 24 \\emph{entire seed blocks}, with resampling seed 20261107. Every draw retains all family, condition, and method observations from a chosen seed. We recompute nonlinear RMSEs and their ratios in each draw; ratios are not averages of per-seed ratios. These 95\\% intervals measure Monte Carlo variability in this fixed benchmark, not unit-level causal uncertainty or generalization to a population of graphs. Condition-level intervals are exploratory and are not multiplicity-adjusted. Absolute-bias intervals are not bias-corrected.\n   153\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/bootstrap]] @evidence[[build_paper_artifacts.py]]\n   154\t\n   155\t\\section{Results}\n   156\t\\subsection{Main Comparison: Improvement Depends on the Comparator}\n   157\tTable~\\ref{tab:main} gives the equal-cell accuracy summaries. The endpoint-tuned estimator's mean RMSE is 0.614, substantially below 1.368 for both assignment-only comparators, but above 0.460 for one-hop OLS. The paired difference relative to one-hop OLS is 0.154, with a 95\\% bootstrap interval [0.127, 0.179]. Its mean absolute bias is also larger than that of one-hop OLS: 0.460 versus 0.393. Thus the aggregate loss is not merely a difference in the direction of signed errors that would cancel when averaged.\n   158\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/rmse]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/endpoint_minus_onehop_rmse_ci]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/absolute_bias]]\n   159\t\n   160\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   161\t\\centering\n   162\t\\caption{Main results: mean cell RMSE and mean cell absolute bias, with 95\\% paired seed-block percentile bootstrap intervals. Lower values are better. These are equal averages of cell summaries, not pooled errors over nodes or cells. Bias intervals include the nonlinearity of taking absolute seed-mean errors.}\n   163\t\\label{tab:main}\n   164\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n   165\t\\toprule\n   166\tEstimator & RMSE [95\\% interval] & Absolute bias [95\\% interval]\\\\\n   167\t\\midrule\n   168\tEndpoint-tuned & 0.614 [0.588, 0.636] & 0.460 [0.431, 0.514]\\\\\n   169\tDifference in means & 1.368 [1.363, 1.371] & 1.363 [1.359, 1.367]\\\\\n   170\tOwn-treatment OLS & 1.368 [1.364, 1.372] & 1.363 [1.360, 1.368]\\\\\n   171\tOne-hop OLS & 0.460 [0.445, 0.474] & 0.393 [0.391, 0.409]\\\\\n   172\t\\bottomrule\n   173\t\\end{tabular}\n   174\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/rmse_ci]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/absolute_bias_ci]]\n   175\t\\end{table}\n   176\t\n   177\tThe endpoint-tuned estimator has lower point-estimated cell RMSE than one-hop OLS in only three of the 24 cells: BA threshold, Stanford3 threshold, and Stanford3 two-hop. This count is descriptive, not a count of statistically established wins. In particular, near-equality of two condition estimates is not sufficient to establish equivalence or superiority. Figure~\\ref{fig:accuracy} presents the complete matrix rather than ranking favorable cases alone.\n   178\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/favorable_cells_vs_onehop]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_rmse]]\n   179\t\n   180\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   181\t\\centering\n   182\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/conditional_accuracy.pdf}\n   183\t\\caption{Condition-specific accuracy across network families. Each point is the endpoint-tuned cell RMSE divided by a comparator's cell RMSE; values below one favor endpoint tuning. Bars are 95\\% seed-block bootstrap intervals on a logarithmic horizontal axis. The same seed draws generate all ratios. Adding spillover adjustment helps relative to assignment-only estimators in strong-linear and cluster conditions, but the linear exposure-aware comparator is usually better.}\n   184\t\\label{fig:accuracy}\n   185\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/ratios]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/ratio_ci]]\n   186\t\\end{figure}\n   187\t\n   188\t\\subsection{Condition Heterogeneity and Misspecification}\n   189\tThe no-spill condition penalizes unnecessary flexibility. Endpoint-tuned RMSE ranges from 0.095 on Stanford3 to 0.594 on SBM, whereas difference-in-means RMSE ranges from 0.019 to 0.051. Even one-hop OLS pays for estimating an unnecessary exposure coefficient here. This comparison does not show that covariate adjustment is generally harmful: the complete references and targets differ, and it is confined to these realized noise, assignment, and topology settings.\n   190\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_rmse]]\n   191\t\n   192\tUnder strong linear spillover with individual assignment, difference-in-means RMSE is approximately two across families, reflecting the large global spillover contribution absent from its arm contrast. Endpoint tuning reduces these errors to 0.773, 0.158, 0.269, and 0.095 for SBM, BA, WS, and Stanford3, respectively. However, one-hop OLS is still better in every family, with corresponding RMSEs 0.194, 0.073, 0.053, and 0.061. Because this condition is linear in the exposure used by OLS, it is a particularly informative simple comparator. A flexible reference and a target-aware objective are not substitutes for a favorable specification.\n   193\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_rmse]]\n   194\t\n   195\tThreshold spillovers expose strong structural heterogeneity. Endpoint-tuned RMSE is 2.373 on SBM, compared with 0.215 on BA, 0.592 on WS, and 0.265 on Stanford3. Its SBM signed bias is $-2.284$. One-hop OLS also has a large SBM threshold RMSE, 1.947, so this is not solely a weakness of the tuning rule. The threshold mechanism is outside the smooth quadratic reference. Sparse or concentrated observed exposure can make the unobserved part of this response important for the global contrast, but the recorded summaries do not isolate support as the cause of the error difference.\n   196\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_rmse]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_signed_bias]]\n   197\t\n   198\tUnder two-hop spillover, both exposure regressions are misspecified. Endpoint-tuned RMSE is 2.085 on SBM, 1.905 on BA, 1.209 on WS, and 1.802 on Stanford3; the corresponding signed biases are $-1.994$, $-1.899$, $-1.179$, and $-1.799$. One-hop OLS likewise has large errors. The global target includes an exposure pathway that the fitted one-hop model omits. Although observed one-hop exposure can correlate with two-hop exposure, this does not ensure accurate prediction when the entire assignment vector changes. The result supports a warning about this omitted mechanism, not a theorem that all one-hop regressions must fail or that any particular multi-hop replacement would succeed.\n   199\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_rmse]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_signed_bias]]\n   200\t\n   201\t\\subsection{Observed Fit and Assignment Design}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
