{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '216,228p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '243,287p'\"","aggregated_output":"   216\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n   217\t\\paragraph{What determines error within this study?}\n   218\tThe comparisons implicate an interaction between the estimator's reference, the interference mechanism, and the assignment distribution. Correctly represented strong linear spillovers favor a simple exposure-aware model over assignment-only analysis. Omitted two-hop spillovers leave large bias in both exposure regressions. A discontinuous response produces markedly different errors across families. These are controlled comparisons of evaluated configurations and prespecified outcome conditions, but not controlled manipulations of every graph property. Degree distribution, community structure, sample size, and exposure support vary together across families. Their isolated causal contributions are not estimated.\n   219\t\n   220\t\\paragraph{Target awareness is not endpoint validation.}\n   221\tThe reference risk calculation concerns noise within an adequate linear model. It cannot detect every discrepancy between that model and the endpoint response. Row-space membership ensures that the reference contrast has an algebraic representation in observed design coordinates; it does not demonstrate observations near all-treated and all-control assignments. Moreover, the selected criterion minimum is subject to reuse of the same outcomes. Neither a recorded small residual nor a finite selected estimate supplies the missing scientific guarantee. We do not claim empirical calibration of the risk criterion because selected penalties, contrast-weight norms, and fallback frequencies were not recorded.\n   222\t\n   223\t\\paragraph{Scope of uncertainty and reproducibility.}\n   224\tThe seed-block bootstrap preserves the paired structure of the evaluation but is based on a limited number of repeated realizations. The fixed Stanford3 graph supports a semi-synthetic topology-specific statement, not external validity for real platform outcomes. All outcomes are generated under the recorded mechanisms and noise scale. There are no confidence-interval coverage measurements, alternative noise stress studies, independently controlled tuning ablations, or evaluations of neural estimators. Absence of usable results for an alternative estimator supplies no evidence of inferior performance. The inspected source and evaluation records are available for audit; association through the selection manifest is not an independently reconstructed executable-identity proof.\n   225\t\n   226\t\\paragraph{Integrity and qualification.}\n   227\tThe final evaluation reports completion and successful task-specific qualification. Constant-outcome, node-relabeling, and reset-replay controls assess limited behavioral properties; their success is not evidence of statistical superiority, arbitrary-interference validity, or model identification. Candidate-produced internal diagnostics are unverified. We rely on evaluator-recorded estimates and retain the negative accuracy comparisons independently of this qualification outcome. Diagnostics not supplied by the evaluator remain unknown.\n   228\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust]]\n   243\t\\appendix\n   244\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n   245\t\\subsection{Complete Condition-Level Accuracy}\n   246\tTable~\\ref{tab:cells} reports all scientific cells. Each entry uses the same 24 seeds and the estimand in Equation~\\ref{eq:target}. Bias is signed for the endpoint-tuned estimator in this table, unlike the absolute-bias aggregate in the main text. Large negative values under two-hop interference reflect underestimation within these simulations. These entries are recomputed from the recorded estimates, not additional scientific evaluations.\n   247\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_rmse]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_signed_bias]]\n   248\t\\begin{table}[h]\n   249\t\\centering\\small\n   250\t\\caption{Complete cell RMSEs and endpoint-tuned signed bias. ET: endpoint-tuned; DM: difference in means; Own: own-treatment OLS; Hop: one-hop OLS. All quantities are in outcome units.}\n   251\t\\label{tab:cells}\n   252\t\\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}\n   253\t\\toprule Family & Condition & ET & DM & Own & Hop & ET bias\\\\\\midrule\n   254\tSBM & No spill & .594 & .051 & .046 & .194 & $-.043$\\\\\n   255\t & Weak linear & .632 & .237 & .235 & .194 & $-.098$\\\\\n   256\t & Strong linear & .773 & 1.982 & 1.982 & .194 & $-.184$\\\\\n   257\t & Threshold & 2.373 & 2.482 & 2.482 & 1.947 & $-2.284$\\\\\n   258\t & Two-hop & 2.085 & 1.940 & 1.940 & 1.919 & $-1.994$\\\\\n   259\t & Cluster & .380 & 1.877 & 1.874 & .145 & $-.085$\\\\\\midrule\n   260\tBA & No spill & .168 & .027 & .027 & .073 & $-.065$\\\\\n   261\t & Weak linear & .169 & .258 & .260 & .073 & $-.066$\\\\\n   262\t & Strong linear & .158 & 2.012 & 2.015 & .073 & $-.053$\\\\\n   263\t & Threshold & .215 & 2.511 & 2.513 & .249 & $-.095$\\\\\n   264\t & Two-hop & 1.905 & 1.821 & 1.823 & 1.825 & $-1.899$\\\\\n   265\t & Cluster & .160 & 1.623 & 1.623 & .099 & $-.069$\\\\\\midrule\n   266\tWS & No spill & .285 & .029 & .024 & .053 & $-.128$\\\\\n   267\t & Weak linear & .277 & .248 & .249 & .053 & $-.121$\\\\\n   268\t & Strong linear & .269 & 1.997 & 1.998 & .053 & $-.108$\\\\\n   269\t & Threshold & .592 & 2.494 & 2.495 & .380 & $-.503$\\\\\n   270\t & Two-hop & 1.209 & 1.797 & 1.798 & 1.065 & $-1.179$\\\\\n   271\t & Cluster & .075 & 1.025 & 1.026 & .062 & $.006$\\\\\\midrule\n   272\tStanford3 & No spill & .095 & .019 & .019 & .061 & $-.001$\\\\\n   273\t & Weak linear & .095 & .250 & .251 & .061 & $-.001$\\\\\n   274\t & Strong linear & .095 & 2.000 & 2.000 & .061 & $-.002$\\\\\n   275\t & Threshold & .265 & 2.500 & 2.500 & .267 & $-.243$\\\\\n   276\t & Two-hop & 1.802 & 1.956 & 1.956 & 1.877 & $-1.799$\\\\\n   277\t & Cluster & .067 & 1.688 & 1.689 & .057 & $-.009$\\\\\n   278\t\\bottomrule\\end{tabular}\n   279\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_rmse]] @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/cell_signed_bias]]\n   280\t\\end{table}\n   281\t\n   282\t\\subsection{Exposure Support and Residual Diagnostics}\n   283\tTable~\\ref{tab:support} summarizes the exposure diagnostics for the matched strong-linear outcome conditions. Minima and maxima are averages of within-realization exposure extremes; endpoint fractions are averages across seeds. The condition number is for the evaluator's simple linear exposure design, not the selected quadratic design. Neither endpoint fractions nor this condition number certifies target identifiability. For Stanford3, endpoint exposures among a minority of nodes do not imply exposure support for every node or subgroup. Full recorded diagnostics are retained in \\texttt{analysis/summary.json}.\n   284\t\\begin{table}[h]\n   285\t\\centering\\small\n   286\t\\caption{Seed-mean observed exposure summaries. Ind.: individual assignment; Clust.: cluster assignment. $p_0,p_1$ are fractions of nodes with exposure exactly zero or one; $\\kappa$ is the recorded linear-design condition number.}\n   287\t\\label{tab:support}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
