{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '287,325p'\"","aggregated_output":"   287\t\\label{tab:support}\n   288\t\\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}\\toprule\n   289\tFamily & Design & Minimum & Maximum & $p_0$ & $p_1$ & $\\kappa$\\\\\\midrule\n   290\tSBM & Ind. & .2372 & .7693 & .0000 & .0000 & 19.02\\\\\n   291\t & Clust. & .0170 & .9822 & .0006 & .0005 & 13.26\\\\\n   292\tBA & Ind. & .0000 & 1.0000 & .0127 & .0134 & 7.59\\\\\n   293\t & Clust. & .0000 & 1.0000 & .0274 & .0329 & 7.70\\\\\n   294\tWS & Ind. & .0000 & .9965 & .0014 & .0015 & 8.81\\\\\n   295\t & Clust. & .0000 & 1.0000 & .1096 & .0917 & 6.68\\\\\n   296\tStanford3 & Ind. & .0000 & 1.0000 & .0324 & .0321 & 9.19\\\\\n   297\t & Clust. & .0000 & 1.0000 & .0489 & .0398 & 7.21\\\\\\bottomrule\n   298\t\\end{tabular}\n   299\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/support]]\n   300\t\\end{table}\n   301\tThe residuals in Figure~\\ref{fig:diagnostics} are observed-assignment in-sample errors, not held-out predictive errors. Even a held-out error at the same assignment distribution would answer a different question from the global intervention contrast unless additional structure connected the two. We do not estimate that connection here.\n   302\t\n   303\t\\subsection{Estimator Specification and Conditional Risk Criterion}\n   304\tThe numerical rank threshold is $\\max(n,p_*)\\epsilon_{\\rm mach}s_{\\max}$ for the standardized non-intercept design with $p_*$ columns. The residual norm of the endpoint contrast outside the retained right-singular-vector span must be at most $10^{-8}\\max(1,\\|q\\|)$. Residual degrees of freedom are $n-r-1$, including the estimated intercept. Nonfinite inputs, failed SVD, unestimable contrast, nonpositive residual degrees of freedom, or no finite risk score trigger the fallback. The fallback uses $[1,x_i^\\top,z_i,e_i,(e_i-.25)_+,(e_i-.5)_+,(e_i-.75)_+,z_ie_i]$, ridge penalty $10^{-3}$ on non-intercept coefficients, and no intercept penalty. Exact equality of all outcomes invokes a constant predictor. These are inspectable source properties; no frequencies of their activation are available.\n   305\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L69-L81]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L119-L158]] @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L199-L213]]\n   306\t\n   307\tFor clarity, write $b_\\lambda=a_\\lambda^\\top\\mu_c-\\gate$ under the correct reference. The fixed-penalty calculation is\n   308\t\\begin{align}\n   309\tE[(\\widehat\\gate_\\lambda-\\widehat\\gate_0)^2]\n   310\t &=b_\\lambda^2+\\sigma^2\\|a_\\lambda-a_0\\|^2,\\\\\n   311\tE[\\widehat R(\\lambda)]\n   312\t &=b_\\lambda^2+\\sigma^2\\|a_\\lambda\\|^2.\n   313\t\\end{align}\n   314\tThe variance correction depends on the same contrast weights as the fitted endpoint predictions. These equations require an unbiased reference and variance estimate and concern a fixed rule conditional on the design. They neither justify reporting the selected minimum as unbiased nor establish an endpoint-risk calibration experiment. We did not record selected penalties, support probabilities, or contrast amplification distributions.\n   315\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/estimator.py#L127-L151]]\n   316\t\n   317\t\\subsection{Benchmark Construction and Reproduction}\n   318\tThe exact archived inputs, rather than regenerated approximations, define this evaluation. Synthetic graphs use NetworkX 3.3 and PCG64 seeds given by the statistical seed plus $100003$ times the one-based family index. The statistical seeds are\n   319\t\\begin{quote}\\small\n   320\t1009, 1013, 1019, 1021, 1031, 1033, 1039, 1049, 1051, 1061, 1063, 1069, 1087, 1091, 1093, 1097, 1103, 1109, 1117, 1123, 1129, 1151, 1153, 1163.\n   321\t\\end{quote}\n   322\tGraphs are normalized to unique undirected edges without self-loops, preserving the declared node set. Graph-specific noise is reused across the outcome conditions, and fitting never receives counterfactual outcomes. The supplied final manifest records graph and raw-row fingerprints. The benchmark archive contains the covariates, assignments, and observed outcomes needed to replay the comparison; a stand-alone raw-input generator is not supplied, so exact regeneration from prose alone is not claimed.\n   323\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/seeds]] @evidence[[source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/generator]] @evidence[[source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/normalization]] @evidence[[reproducibility/resources/final/benchmark.npz]]\n   324\t\n   325\tThe declared research environment is NumPy 1.26.4, SciPy 1.13.1, and NetworkX 3.3 under Python 3.11 or 3.12. The evaluation allows at most two CPU cores and 8 GiB per role, with numerical library threads set to one. Its final wall-clock allowance is 7,200 seconds; the recorded complete evaluation elapsed time is about 629 seconds, not a per-estimator runtime. No comparative speed claim follows. A replay requires the evaluator's supported Linux isolation facilities. \\texttt{REPRODUCIBILITY.md} records the checked entrypoints and distinguishes replay commands, which were not rerun for this manuscript, from the derived-analysis command that was executed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
