\"Own-treatment OLS\": 1.6894885441559382, \"One-hop OLS\": 0.05717915786006032, \"endpoint_signed_bias\": -0.009049808721266372, \"mean_observed_fit_rmse\": 0.9996903758946961} SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n31: Observed-assignment fitted residual RMSE spans [0.9762526322574112, 1.0808042947319605], near unit noise, while threshold and two-hop endpoint errors are substantial; this is descriptive, not causal. SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n32: SBM Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.0, \"fraction_zero\": 0.0, \"linear_design_condition\": 19.02354932593077, \"maximum\": 0.7693330536163302, \"minimum\": 0.23716191363617892} SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n33: SBM Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.000518798828125, \"fraction_zero\": 0.0006103515625, \"linear_design_condition\": 13.26309780585197, \"maximum\": 0.9822164144943205, \"minimum\": 0.01698245843747851} SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n34: BA Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.013427734375, \"fraction_zero\": 0.012715657552083334, \"linear_design_condition\": 7.588901196448447, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0} SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n35: BA Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.032928466796875, \"fraction_zero\": 0.027394612630208332, \"linear_design_condition\": 7.7038367703447435, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0} SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n36: WS Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.0015157063802083333, \"fraction_zero\": 0.0014241536458333333, \"linear_design_condition\": 8.807364526266976, \"maximum\": 0.9965277777777777, \"minimum\": 0.0} SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n37: WS Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.09168497721354167, \"fraction_zero\": 0.10963948567708333, \"linear_design_condition\": 6.682392671890018, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0} SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n38: Stanford3 Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.03205736808792221, \"fraction_zero\": 0.03242059382012774, \"linear_design_condition\": 9.193799507457536, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0} SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n39: Stanford3 Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.03979299729558663, \"fraction_zero\": 0.04890960354450774, \"linear_design_condition\": 7.208791238224571, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0} SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n40: Ratio plots divide cell RMSEs and bootstrap complete seed blocks. The resampling uses 10000 draws, seed 20261107, and 95% percentile intervals. Intervals quantify fixed-benchmark Monte Carlo variation, not causal coverage or graph-population generalization. SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n41: The benchmark uses 4096-node synthetic SBM, BA and WS graphs, and fixed Stanford3 with 11586 nodes and 568309 undirected edges. SBM uses four blocks with probabilities .04/.002; BA attachment is five; WS neighborhood is ten with rewiring .15. Covariates have one column. Synthetic topologies vary by seed and Stanford3 topology does not. SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n42: The outcome model is .5*x + z + spillover + unit Gaussian noise. Six spillovers are 0, .25*e, 2*e, 2*I(e>=.75)+z*e, 2*h, and 2*e under cluster assignment. Recorded GATEs are respectively 1, 1.25, 3, 4, 3, 3. Two-hop exposure includes backtracking; isolates have zero exposure. SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n43: Individual assignment is Bernoulli(.5). The cluster design uses 32 deterministic balanced graph-BFS clusters and independent Bernoulli(.5) cluster assignment; it changes the joint assignment distribution rather than exposure support alone. SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n44: The inspected associated estimator has quadratic one-hop features, saved observed-data standardization, SVD, endpoint-contrast risk grid n*{0,1e-6,1e-4,1e-2,1}, and deterministic minimum selection. Source association is not an independently reconstructed executable-identity proof. SOURCES: result.best_correct_score,final.evidence.episodes\n45: The fixed-penalty risk expression equals squared bias plus contrast variance only under the stated correct-reference and noise assumptions. The selected minimum is not an unbiased selected-risk estimate; empirical calibration is unmeasured. SOURCES: result.best_correct_score\n46: Rank tolerance is max(design shape)*machine epsilon*smax, contrast residual tolerance 1e-8*max(1,norm(q)), residual df n-r-1. The fallback uses hinges .25,.5,.75, ridge 1e-3, and unpenalized intercept. Exact-constant outcomes have zero contrast. Fallback and penalty frequencies are unavailable. SOURCES: result.best_correct_score\n47: The three substantive comparators are difference in means, covariate-adjusted own-treatment OLS and linear one-hop OLS, evaluated on matched cases. OLS endpoint contrast accounts for nonisolated-node fraction. SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n48: Component ablations, same-reference GCV comparison, original-hinge matched final comparison, inference coverage, risk calibration and additional noise studies are absent; missing measurements do not establish inferior performance. SOURCES: final.evidence.episodes\n49: Recorded control success is 1 and failed-episode fraction 0. Qualification and limited behavioral controls do not establish arbitrary-interference validity; candidate internal diagnostics are unverified. SOURCES: final.metric.control_success_fraction,final.metric.failed_episode_fraction,final.qualification.outcome\n50: The evaluator caps signed errors at magnitude 10 and normalizes by unit noise; it averages cell RMSEs and absolute biases equally, not pooled squared errors. SOURCES: final.metric.mean_capped_normalized_rmse,final.metric.mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias,final.evidence.episodes\n51: Reproduction uses archived PCG64-generated inputs, seed+100003*(family ordinal+1), the listed 24 seeds, declared NumPy1.26.4/SciPy1.13.1/NetworkX3.3, two CPU cores and 8GiB per role, 7200s wall allowance. Recorded elapsed evaluation is approximately629s, not a per-method runtime. SOURCES: final.qualification.status\n52: Literature context only: Estimating average causal effects under general interference, with application to a social network experiment. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Aronow2017\n53: Literature context only: Graph cluster randomization: network exposure to multiple universes. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Ugander2013\n54: Literature context only: Design and Analysis of Experiments in Networks: Reducing Bias from Interference. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Eckles2017\n55: Literature context only: Regression Adjustments for Estimating the Global Treatment Effect in Experiments with Interference. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Chin2019\n56: Literature context only: Model-based regression adjustment with model-free covariates for network interference. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Han2023\n57: Literature context only: Network Experimentation at Scale. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Karrer2021\n58: Literature context only: Staggered Rollout Designs Enable Causal Inference Under Interference Without Network Knowledge. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Cortez2022\n59: Literature context only: Social structure of Facebook networks. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Original document abstract and introduction; no citation graph access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Traud2012\n60: Literature context only: The Network Data Repository with Interactive Graph Analytics and Visualization. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Rossi2015\n61: Literature context only: Stochastic blockmodels with a growing number of classes. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Choi2012\n62: Literature context only: Emergence of Scaling in Random Networks. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Barabasi1999\n63: Literature context only: Collective dynamics of ‘small-world’ networks. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Europe PMC abstract; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Watts1998\n64: Literature context only: Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Hoerl1970\n65: Literature context only: Generalized Cross-Validation as a Method for Choosing a Good Ridge Parameter. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Golub1979\n66: Literature context only: Estimation of the Mean of a Multivariate Normal Distribution. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access SOURCES: ref.openalex.Stein1981\n67: Literature context only: H