{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"jq -r '.claims|to_entries[]|select(.key>=31 and .key<=52)|\\\"\\\\(.key): \\\\(.value.claim)\\\\nREFS: \\\\(.value.evidence_refs|join(\\\", \\\"))\\\"' claims_grounding.json; jq '{generator,mechanisms,normalization,aggregation,seeds,attribution,time_plan,controls}' source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json\"","aggregated_output":"31: Observed-assignment fitted residual RMSE spans [0.9762526322574112, 1.0808042947319605], near unit noise, while threshold and two-hop endpoint errors are substantial; this is descriptive, not causal.\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/fit_rmse_range, analysis/observations.csv\n32: SBM Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.0, \"fraction_zero\": 0.0, \"linear_design_condition\": 19.02354932593077, \"maximum\": 0.7693330536163302, \"minimum\": 0.23716191363617892}\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/support\n33: SBM Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.000518798828125, \"fraction_zero\": 0.0006103515625, \"linear_design_condition\": 13.26309780585197, \"maximum\": 0.9822164144943205, \"minimum\": 0.01698245843747851}\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/support\n34: BA Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.013427734375, \"fraction_zero\": 0.012715657552083334, \"linear_design_condition\": 7.588901196448447, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/support\n35: BA Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.032928466796875, \"fraction_zero\": 0.027394612630208332, \"linear_design_condition\": 7.7038367703447435, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/support\n36: WS Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.0015157063802083333, \"fraction_zero\": 0.0014241536458333333, \"linear_design_condition\": 8.807364526266976, \"maximum\": 0.9965277777777777, \"minimum\": 0.0}\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/support\n37: WS Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.09168497721354167, \"fraction_zero\": 0.10963948567708333, \"linear_design_condition\": 6.682392671890018, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/support\n38: Stanford3 Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.03205736808792221, \"fraction_zero\": 0.03242059382012774, \"linear_design_condition\": 9.193799507457536, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/support\n39: Stanford3 Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.03979299729558663, \"fraction_zero\": 0.04890960354450774, \"linear_design_condition\": 7.208791238224571, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/support\n40: Ratio plots divide cell RMSEs and bootstrap complete seed blocks. The resampling uses 10000 draws, seed 20261107, and 95% percentile intervals. Intervals quantify fixed-benchmark Monte Carlo variation, not causal coverage or graph-population generalization.\nREFS: analysis/summary.json#/bootstrap, build_paper_artifacts.py\n41: The benchmark uses 4096-node synthetic SBM, BA and WS graphs, and fixed Stanford3 with 11586 nodes and 568309 undirected edges. SBM uses four blocks with probabilities .04/.002; BA attachment is five; WS neighborhood is ten with rewiring .15. Covariates have one column. Synthetic topologies vary by seed and Stanford3 topology does not.\nREFS: source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/generator, source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/records, reproducibility/resources/final/benchmark.npz\n42: The outcome model is .5*x + z + spillover + unit Gaussian noise. Six spillovers are 0, .25*e, 2*e, 2*I(e>=.75)+z*e, 2*h, and 2*e under cluster assignment. Recorded GATEs are respectively 1, 1.25, 3, 4, 3, 3. Two-hop exposure includes backtracking; isolates have zero exposure.\nREFS: source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L676-L694, source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/mechanisms, paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n43: Individual assignment is Bernoulli(.5). The cluster design uses 32 deterministic balanced graph-BFS clusters and independent Bernoulli(.5) cluster assignment; it changes the joint assignment distribution rather than exposure support alone.\nREFS: source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/mechanisms\n44: The inspected associated estimator has quadratic one-hop features, saved observed-data standardization, SVD, endpoint-contrast risk grid n*{0,1e-6,1e-4,1e-2,1}, and deterministic minimum selection. Source association is not an independently reconstructed executable-identity proof.\nREFS: source_evidence/estimator.py#L83-L160, source_evidence/program_spec.toml\n45: The fixed-penalty risk expression equals squared bias plus contrast variance only under the stated correct-reference and noise assumptions. The selected minimum is not an unbiased selected-risk estimate; empirical calibration is unmeasured.\nREFS: source_evidence/estimator.py#L127-L151\n46: Rank tolerance is max(design shape)*machine epsilon*smax, contrast residual tolerance 1e-8*max(1,norm(q)), residual df n-r-1. The fallback uses hinges .25,.5,.75, ridge 1e-3, and unpenalized intercept. Exact-constant outcomes have zero contrast. Fallback and penalty frequencies are unavailable.\nREFS: source_evidence/estimator.py#L69-L81, source_evidence/estimator.py#L119-L158, source_evidence/estimator.py#L199-L213\n47: The three substantive comparators are difference in means, covariate-adjusted own-treatment OLS and linear one-hop OLS, evaluated on matched cases. OLS endpoint contrast accounts for nonisolated-node fraction.\nREFS: source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L371-L392\n48: Component ablations, same-reference GCV comparison, original-hinge matched final comparison, inference coverage, risk calibration and additional noise studies are absent; missing measurements do not establish inferior performance.\nREFS: paper_data.json#/ablation_studies, paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n49: Recorded control success is 1 and failed-episode fraction 0. Qualification and limited behavioral controls do not establish arbitrary-interference validity; candidate internal diagnostics are unverified.\nREFS: paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics, paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust\n50: The evaluator caps signed errors at magnitude 10 and normalizes by unit noise; it averages cell RMSEs and absolute biases equally, not pooled squared errors.\nREFS: source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/aggregation, analysis/summary.json\n51: Reproduction uses archived PCG64-generated inputs, seed+100003*(family ordinal+1), the listed 24 seeds, declared NumPy1.26.4/SciPy1.13.1/NetworkX3.3, two CPU cores and 8GiB per role, 7200s wall allowance. Recorded elapsed evaluation is approximately629s, not a per-method runtime.\nREFS: source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/seeds, source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/generator, source_evidence/requirements.txt, source_evidence/benchmark_manifest.json#/time_plan, source_evidence/final_manifest.json#/compute_usage\n52: Literature context only: Estimating average causal effects under general interference, with application to a social network experiment. Claims are restricted to the documented reading scope: Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nREFS: reference_evidence/Aronow2017.txt\n{\n  \"generator\": \"NetworkX 3.3; four-block SBM p_in=.04 p_out=.002; BA m=5; WS k=10 rewire=.15. RNG PCG64 with seed +100003*(family ordinal+1). Independent graph/observation statistical seeds across planes.\",\n  \"mechanisms\": \"tau=1; x coefficient .5; unit fixed Gaussian noise sd1. M0 no spill; M1 .25*neighbor mean; M2 2*neighbor mean; M3 2*I(mean>=.75)+z*mean; M4 2*mean(neighbor means), length-two random walks with backtracking; M5 M2 with 32 deterministic balanced graph-BFS clusters: start smallest unassigned node, traverse sorted CSR neighbors, assign up to ceil(n/32), restart at smallest unassigned when frontier empties, discard frontier between clusters. Independent cluster p=.5. Isolate neighbor means=0. Not claimed optimal clustering.\",\n  \"normalization\": \"Undirected sorted int32 unique pairs, remove self-loops, preserve declared n/isolates; float64 little endian and signed zero normalized. IDs excluded. Relabel-invariant conservative structural-context multiset graph fingerprint; rows hash raw x,z,y plus sorted neighbor-degree context. Hash collisions are conservative rejection, not accepted disjointness. No text examples.\",\n  \"aggregation\": {\n    \"scientific\": \"equal seeds within each case, equal six mechanisms within each family, equal families; all original slots retained\",\n    \"weights\": {\n      \"scientific\": 0.8,\n      \"constant\": 0.1,\n      \"permutation\": 0.05,\n      \"replay\": 0.05\n    },\n    \"smoke_weights\": {\n      \"M0\": 0.4,\n      \"M2\": 0.4,\n      \"constant\": 0.1,\n      \"permutation\": 0.05,\n      \"replay\": 0.05\n    },\n    \"utility\": \"1-min(10,max(-10,estimate-true_gate))**2/100; noise scale=1\",\n    \"rmse\": \"sqrt(mean(clipped_error**2 over seeds)) then equal cases/families\",\n    \"bias\": \"abs(mean(signed clipped_error over seeds)) then equal cases/families; incomplete case contributes 10\",\n    \"failure\": {\n      \"utility\": 0,\n      \"signed_error\": 10,\n      \"squared_error\": 100,\n      \"control_success\": 0\n    },\n    \"control_metric_weights\": {\n      \"constant\": 0.5,\n      \"permutation\": 0.25,\n      \"replay\": 0.25\n    },\n    \"evidence_seed_score\": \"same hierarchy within seed, common sole replay contribution .05 shared across seed records; mean seed scores equals combined_score\",\n    \"failure_denominator\": \"161 search, 625 final, 5 smoke; unsuccessful matched controls count as failed episodes\"\n  },\n  \"seeds\": [\n    1009,\n    1013,\n    1019,\n    1021,\n    1031,\n    1033,\n    1039,\n    1049,\n    1051,\n    1061,\n    1063,\n    1069,\n    1087,\n    1091,\n    1093,\n    1097,\n    1103,\n    1109,\n    1117,\n    1123,\n    1129,\n    1151,\n    1153,\n    1163\n  ],\n  \"attribution\": \"Stanford3: Rossi & Ahmed (AAAI 2015), Traud, Mucha & Porter (Physica A 2012). Network Repository CC BY-SA as verified in canonical task; version not specified. Retain attribution and share-alike obligations. Archive checksum and 11586/568309 dimensions verified; no SNAP acquisition or restricted literature access.\",\n  \"time_plan\": {\n    \"wall_seconds\": 7200,\n    \"startup_allowance_seconds\": 180,\n    \"finish_reserve_seconds\": 30,\n    \"reset_timeout_seconds\": 1.0,\n    \"episode_overhead_seconds\": 2.7,\n    \"cleanup_derivation\": \"Candidate terminate .1 + kill/reap .2 + stderr reader reserve 1.0 =1.3; baseline same 1.3; bookkeeping .1 =2.7. Reader actually closes within .4, leaving conservative headroom; no repeated waits after reaping.\",\n    \"matched_work_seconds_per_method\": 1.2,\n    \"methods\": 6,\n    \"method_bookkeeping_seconds\": 0.01,\n    \"productive_time_floor\": null,\n    \"calibration\": \"No canonical productive-work minimum or threshold delegation exists. Work allowances bound actual fixed dimensions; do not sleep, inflate runtime or tune scientific thresholds.\",\n    \"platform\": \"Linux x86_64, CPython 3.11 or 3.12; native smoke needs Landlock ABI>=3 and libseccomp.so.2; no namespace privilege required. All numerical-library threads one, at most two cores per role; 8 GiB per role; no GPUs. Harness enforces production role CPU/memory isolation.\"\n  },\n  \"controls\": \"constant y=3.25 on SBM M2, nonidentity reversal of all SBM M2 rows and edges, reset replay first scientific episode; within-plane reuse is intentional. Tolerances atol=rtol=1e-6.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
